ARM: dts: omap5: add the intermediate operating points below 1.5 GHz

The Letux Cortex 15 device tree gives the Pyra six CPU operating points,
250 MHz apart from 250 MHz to 1.5 GHz.  Six steps across a six-fold range
is coarse, and it costs this board twice.  The cpufreq floor sits at
250 MHz, six steps below the top, so every interaction pays a full ramp;
and `ondemand` on this machine is configured to step up only at 95% load,
so the ramp it does take is slow as well as long.

Add the five steps that sit between the existing ones.  Every original
step keeps its original voltage to the microvolt, and the new ones are
placed on the same curve between them:

	 375 MHz  0.800 V
	 625 MHz  0.900 V
	 875 MHz  1.000 V
	1125 MHz  1.080 V
	1400 MHz  1.200 V

Nothing here runs faster than the OMAP5432's 1.5 GHz rating, and the top
voltage is unchanged, so this does not raise the ceiling in either sense.

Every voltage above has to be one the supply can actually produce.  The
points drive smps123, a Palmas SMPS whose low range is 510000 uV plus a
multiple of 10000, so 10 mV is the granularity and anything off that grid
is rejected at boot by _opp_supported_by_regulators, which drops the
whole point with a single line in the log and leaves a table quietly
shorter than the one in the source.  The published table this is taken
from asks 1.075 V at 1125 MHz and loses that point exactly that way,
confirmed on hardware.  1.080 V is the next step up and is used here
instead; rounding up rather than down, because the voltage is there to
hold the frequency.  The supply is constrained to 1.5 V in
omap5-board-common.dtsi, and every voltage above is well inside that.

The table is otherwise as published by a Pyra owner who had tuned and run
it, read back out of the blob rather than retyped; see
experiments/thermals-and-frequency/README.md for how it was verified.
That same blob carries five further points above the part's rating, which
are deliberately not in this patch.

This is an experiment and it is not part of the Pyra fix set.  The
voltages are somebody else's measurements on somebody else's unit, and a
point whose voltage is too low for its frequency produces a machine that
is unstable rather than one that refuses to boot.  Build it under its own
release name, boot it one-shot so that it reverts by itself, and measure
it against improvements.md item 1 before carrying it any further.

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-common.dtsi
@@ -76,13 +76,24 @@
 };
 
 &cpu0 {
+	/*
+	 * Five intermediate steps between the six the board shipped with.
+	 * Every original step keeps its original voltage; the new ones sit
+	 * on the same curve between them.  Nothing here runs faster than
+	 * the part is rated for.
+	 */
 	operating-points = <
 		/* kHz    uV */
 		250000 750000
+		375000 800000
 		500000 850000
+		625000 900000
 		750000 950000
+		875000 1000000
 		1000000 1050000
+		1125000 1080000
 		1250000 1100000
+		1400000 1200000
 		1500000 1250000
 #if 0	/* potentially overclocking */
 		1700000 1250000
